<!-- awf:section positioning -->
# {{ .prefix }}-executing-plans

The inline implementation chain iterates plan phases. Inline is selected per phase, so a mixed plan can hand subagent-driven phases to its companion.

<!-- awf:include plan-flexibility -->
<!-- awf:end -->

<!-- awf:section when-to-invoke -->
## When to invoke

Invoke for an accepted plan with an `inline` phase. Read every phase's declared execution mode; do not infer one mode for the whole plan.
<!-- awf:end -->

<!-- awf:section procedure-resolve-plan -->
## Procedure

<!-- awf:include outline-approval -->

1. Resolve the mutable plan and read its complete phase structure. This skill may run without an effort. If continuity independently fired, validate its immutable slug and exact `.awf/efforts/<slug>/memory.md`, confirm canonical YAML `effort: <slug>` identity, preserve one user-managed writer, and use the managed worktree when present; otherwise omit effort and memory fields. If changed facts newly make continuity useful, invoke `{{ if index .skills "effort-workflow" }}{{ .prefix }}-effort-workflow{{ else }}the effort lifecycle owner{{ end }}` before further mutation. A fresh owner assigned one phase or task may run `./awf read plan <plan> <P[.T]>` for its executable closure, including the generated task scope notice, phase-owned Advances and Completes outcomes, Notes, and Phase close; all are phase-owner context only; projection changes neither phase ownership nor checkpoint boundaries and never gives a task helper commit, review, checkpoint, handoff, or outcome authority. {{if eq .profile "core"}}Project documentation, source, tests, and repository history remain authoritative{{else}}Repository sources and current-state documentation remain authoritative{{end}}, and standalone memory is forbidden.
<!-- awf:end -->

<!-- awf:section procedure-raise-concerns -->
2. You, the parent executing this plan, resolve a missing or stale phase path, check, closing subject, or local instruction when repository authority determines it. Amend the mutable plan and record the reasoned deviation in Notes only when another phase or reviewer can rely on that material instruction, then continue. Use the shared authority boundary when a genuine unresolved design fork remains.
<!-- awf:end -->

<!-- awf:section procedure-per-task -->
3. **Iterate phases, not tasks.** Per `{{ .layout.maintainableCodeDesign }}`, you preserve the plan's settled durable choices, including a bounded enabling refactor; reassess if grounded source contradicts them, and stop rather than bolt correctness onto the wrong abstraction.
<!-- awf:include clean-integration -->
<!-- awf:include semantic-owner-assurance -->
<!-- awf:include implementation-autonomy -->
You run `./awf context <the phase's exact paths>` before editing.
<!-- awf:include context-spill -->
**Rule.** For an `inline` phase, the parent owns every ordered task, integration, staged check, every commit, fast gate, terminal exhaustive verification, report-only phase review, focused settlement commit, and checkpoint.

**Flexible helper detail.** A batch may dispatch explicitly partitioned sequential commit-disabled helpers only. Each helper is the `implementer` agent in commit-disabled mode, and its rendered contract governs path confinement, green obligation, escalation, and return shape. Each brief explicitly identifies the parent-supplied approved boundary. When an effort exists, the brief carries its slug and exact owned memory path as read-only context; helpers never edit shared memory.

**Runtime detail.** {{ if .targetSubagentTools }}Effort-backed pre-integration implementation dispatches supply the managed-worktree path as `verificationCheckout`; effort-free or root-owned work intentionally omits it. The validated checkout is both the implementation child CWD and before-and-after HEAD identity. The main Pi session remains at the project root, so parent mutations name managed-worktree paths explicitly. Child CWD alignment is not filesystem confinement. Governed primary-checkout lifecycle transitions remain at the primary checkout. Choose the smallest reliable tier - `small` (narrow, mechanical), `standard` (substantive but bounded), or `large` (broad, intricate, cross-cutting, or high-consequence) - escalating after uncertainty, failed reasoning, or widened scope; omit the `model` field to use configured role routing, overriding deliberately with the tier's exact `provider/model-id`. Never pass `default`, `auto`, or `inherit parent` as a model value.{{ else }}Choose the smallest reliable tier - `small` (narrow, mechanical), `standard` (substantive but bounded), or `large` (broad, intricate, cross-cutting, or high-consequence) - escalating after uncertainty, failed reasoning, or widened scope; select the smallest reliable target-native model explicitly, or use the harness default and note in the dispatch brief that explicit selection is unavailable.{{ end }} Full tier definitions: docs/workflow.md.

**Required helper evidence.** Each commit-disabled implementation child returns a closed receipt with assigned scope, canonical checkout, starting and ending HEAD and `git status --short`, changed paths, each exact focused command with cwd, argv, exit status, and actual result, plus whether it ran after the last mutation to every overlapping path, completed and remaining work, deviations, separately routed blockers, and applicable generated-output or fixture evidence. You independently inventory each return, reject out-of-subset writes, retain shared-file ownership, confirm unchanged HEAD and reported end state, run the deterministic post-check, and never delegate integration, staging, checks, commits, or gates. Reuse only explicitly final-state focused results while checkout identity, relevant authority, and overlapping paths remain unchanged; lost or unverifiable evidence, changed relevant authority, or later overlapping mutation invalidates the affected receipt, and every non-final result is rerun.

**Required phase evidence.** For generated-prose changes, perform the focused meaning review at the produced-output boundaries and retain the inspected boundaries and result as completion evidence. The parent stages the complete transaction and creates the phase-closing commit only after `./awf check staged` and {{ with .vars.gateCmd }}`{{ . }}`{{ else }}the project's gate{{ end }} pass; a wired pre-commit hook enforces both at commit time, and the parent runs them manually first only in a clone without wired hooks (checkable with `git config core.hooksPath`; when in doubt, run both manually). The parent performs terminal exhaustive verification after implementation completes. A receipt never substitutes for either parent-owned gate.

Before review, build the phase-review brief from the exact phase-closing commit and range, complete phase scope, verification results, the verbatim deviation report built from the parent's inventory, and any unreviewed settlement. Then dispatch report-only phase review through correctness, plan/authority, documentation, and maintainability lenses. Its structured coverage summary returns those inputs, the reviewed range, and freshness against the current branch tip.

**Freshness stop.** Reuse coverage only after validating branch-tip freshness. Evidence loss after context loss, session replacement, or effort-free continuation, and any unverifiable freshness, falls back to ordinary terminal review. Divergence, changed authority, reasoned post-review fixes, or material mutation invalidates affected coverage and triggers renewed review. Review settles before checkpointing. The parent owns this transient evidence and may persist only a concise advisory observation.

<!-- awf:include checkpoint-routine -->
<!-- awf:include phase-loop-continuation -->

If an implementer stops dirty, first inventory `git status --short`, diff, completed and remaining work, and failed checks. Choose exactly one: complete inline; restore the known green baseline and restart the complete revised phase; or stop for required user input. An explicit ownership transfer includes the complete revised phase, inventory, completed and remaining work, and recovery verification. Never issue a blind successor instruction for one task or dispatch over a dirty checkout.
<!-- awf:end -->

<!-- awf:section tdd-opt-in -->
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section gate-tier-detail -->
{{ with .data.e2eSuitePaths }}Use the fuller gate when a phase touches a declared end-to-end surface: {{ range . }}`{{ .path }}` {{ end }}.{{ end }}
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section procedure-adr-final-commit -->
4. Apply every explicit batch of the ADR's declared State changes operations (V2), including the final batch, atomically with its matching claims and lifecycle event in the owning phase. Do not flip terminal artifact status in a phase.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section procedure-non-adr-final-commit -->
5. Keep the plan mutable through implementation; its terminal status changes only in the deferred artifact transaction.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section terminal-step -->
6. After all settled phases, reuse only fresh phase coverage without rereading already-covered phase correctness: a single phase reviews unreviewed settlement or integration, while multiple phases focus cross-phase composition, settlements, and integration. Always retain `./awf audit` over the complete final implementation range, including settlement commits. Evaluate implementation-review need independently. Invoke `{{ .prefix }}-reviewing-impl` for meaningful breadth, non-obvious logic, contract or compatibility effects, migrations, security, concurrency, data-loss risk, verification requiring substantive judgment, or uncertainty. Skip it only for locally obvious, low-risk, directly verified work where independent review is unlikely to find meaningful hidden consequences. After assurance settles or is explicitly skipped, effort-backed work returns to `{{ if index .skills "effort-workflow" }}{{ .prefix }}-effort-workflow{{ else }}the effort lifecycle owner{{ end }}` for artifact closure. For effort-free work, the parent performs the deferred ADR/plan terminal transaction only after appending each closing Proposed plan's parsed Terminal reconciliation under Notes, using a full-SHA implementation range ending at current `HEAD`, the exact old and new touched paths accumulated across every selected commit including merge resolutions, and every material route deviation or exact `none`; an endpoint diff or prose markers cannot substitute. It applies only legal status transitions under `{{ .prefix }}-adr-lifecycle`, runs the required gate and audit, and concludes.
<!-- awf:end -->

<!-- awf:section project-invariants -->
## Notes
- Expected output is non-negotiable. Never weaken assertions or adjust a golden merely to pass.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section notes-gate -->
- Gates are mandatory; fix the root cause.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section notes-auto-commit -->
- The parent commits the integrated phase only after its staged check and fast gate are green.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section notes-one-concern -->
- One coherent phase concern per closing commit.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section notes-docs-travel -->
- Docs travel with behavior.
<!-- awf:end -->
<!-- awf:section red-flags -->
## Red flags

Do not turn ordered tasks into transaction boundaries, let helpers commit, or checkpoint before report-only review settlement.
<!-- awf:end -->
